WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.795
A catalog of patterns, and the trade they all make.

2
00:00:03.075 --> 00:00:16.109
A June 2025 paper by Beurer-Kellner and colleagues, with authors from several labs and companies, generalizes this into six design patterns for agents with resistance to prompt injection.

3
00:00:16.389 --> 00:00:25.712
In brief, in this course’s words: Action-selector: the model only picks from a fixed menu of actions and never sees tool output; like a smart router.

4
00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:34.525
Plan-then-execute: the plan is fixed before untrusted data is read, so data can’t add new actions (though it can still affect arguments).

5
00:00:34.805 --> 00:00:48.523
LLM map-reduce: each untrusted item is processed by an isolated sub-agent that returns a constrained result (a boolean, a number, a fixed-schema record), and only those results are combined.

6
00:00:48.803 --> 00:00:54.541
Dual LLM: privileged and quarantined models with symbolic variables, as above.

7
00:00:54.821 --> 00:01:03.205
Code-then-execute: the CaMeL approach, a program written from the trusted query and run by an interpreter that enforces policies.

8
00:01:03.485 --> 00:01:13.597
Context minimization: remove untrusted or unnecessary content (including the user’s original prompt, when it might carry an injection) before later steps.

9
00:01:13.877 --> 00:01:26.933
Willison’s summary of the paper highlights its framing: these patterns deliberately limit what agents can do, and the authors doubt that general-purpose agents can offer reliable safety guarantees with current models.

10
00:01:27.213 --> 00:01:31.181
Every pattern trades flexibility for a property you can reason about.

11
00:01:31.461 --> 00:01:34.277
For the project agent, two patterns fit naturally.

12
00:01:34.557 --> 00:01:48.403
Map-reduce for reading issues: a cheap model turns each issue into a fixed-schema record (status, owner, blocked or not, a short summary with no URLs), and only the records reach the step that writes the report.

13
00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:59.797
Plan-then-execute for actions: the set of actions is fixed by your code (draft a report, request approval, post to one channel), and nothing in an issue can add “email someone.

14
00:02:00.077 --> 00:02:11.107
” Note the model-selection angle: the per-issue step is high volume and low judgment, which suits a small, inexpensive model, and its constrained output is what makes that safe.
